The Stranger's storm, redrawn: mockup round 4 (waiting for .go before wiring)

Branch stills/stranger-storm-art, folder stranger-storm-art/round4/ (earlier rounds stay beside it). GBA style only (Eric's pick, B):
  storm-phone-ice-turn.gif / .mp4        phone, light, the storm's 1st turn (ice leads), the rift whole
  storm-phone-fire-turn.gif / .mp4       phone, light, the 2nd turn (fire leads), the rift closing
  storm-computer-ice-turn.gif / .mp4     computer, light, the 1st turn
  storm-phone-three-turns.png            the three turns side by side: the rift whole, closing, nearly healed
  storm-computer-ice-turn.png            computer, full screen
The MP4s and PNGs show the true pixels; the GIFs are scaled down (their pixels look softer).

The rift, redrawn to Eric's 18:45 note (scripts/storm-art/rift.py)
- One central impact between his swords: a dark pit with a white-hot seam where the two sides meet, board chunks flying out from it
  (the board's two colors, each with its thickness, a rim lit by its side's glow and a shadow that is farther off the higher it flew).
- A wide lava fissure to the lower left: running lava on its floor, basalt walls with veins, scorch and molten rock over its lips,
  flames rising out of it. A wide ice fissure to the upper right: a deep blue floor with pale facets, ice walls, rime on its lips,
  clusters of crystals erupting from both lips. Thinner branches off each, and hairline cracks past those.
- Real depth, the way a top-down pixel game draws a pit: under every upper lip you see the board's own thickness, then the wall going
  down into the dark, then the floor far below with the wall's shadow across it. The lips are lifted slabs (lit at the edge, shaded
  toward a break line). Each side's glow spills softly onto the squares.
- GBA style: the ring's own pixel size, one 15-color palette, ordered dithering, hard see-through levels.
- It lives: a 4-frame loop at 6 a second (the flames lick, the lava and crystals glint, the chunks bob a pixel).
- Three heal stages, one each turn: whole; 72% as long and 74% as wide with fewer chunks and crystals and lower flames; 44% and half
  as wide; then gone with the storm. Under the pieces; the last-move highlight and a fire turn's marked tiles stay over it.
- Known, to fix when wiring: at stages 2 and 3 the ice fissure's thin far end reads pale; it should be a dark hairline.

The rest is round 2's B: the ring (36 frames, 250 px, 12 a second), the soft dim (22%), the storm see-through (88%).

What would ship: storm.webp 376 KB + frost.webp 1 KB + three rift strips 17 KB in all (about 12 MB decoded for the ring's loop).
At play time: 2 to 6 image draws a frame for the ring, no particles; the rift one small image stepped by CSS; the dim one CSS layer.

Calls for the director / Eric
1. The rift's look and size (its fissures are about a square wide near the impact).
2. In the opening scene the rift would open as the swords go in (in place of today's line cracks) and stay; the ripples stay.
3. The painterly files (round 3's A) stay in the scripts (`soft`), unused, unless you want them dropped.
